<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manusmriti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 200 BCE–200 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Manusmṛti, Mānava-Dharmaśāstra, Manava Dharmashastra, Laws of Manu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Manusmriti, also known as the Manava <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmashastra">Dharmashastra</a> or the Laws of Manu, is a major legal text among the Dharmashastras of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> work in verse that most scholars date to between 200 BCE and 200 CE. It presents itself as a discourse by Manu and Bhrigu on duties, laws, conduct, gender norms and virtues, and is best known for its different rules for the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a>. Its influence reached Hindu kingdoms in what are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, and it was used to construct the Hindu law code of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s enclaves. In modern times it is controversial for its treatment of women and lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> burned copies in 1927 to protest caste discrimination.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chenla's kings took the Sanskrit name of a patron deity or avatar with the suffix -varman, &quot;protected by&quot;, following the code of the Kshatriya caste in the <b>Laws of Manu</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The law books, of which the <b>Manusmriti</b> is the most famous, set out the duties of the classes and stages of life.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…1789; the Puranas, encyclopedic collections of myth and history; the treatises on law and statecraft, such as the <b>Laws of Manu</b> and the Arthashastra; the Kamasutra; the theory of drama and aesthetic emotion of the Natyashastra; and the animal fables of the Panchatantra, which travelled through Middle Persian…</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Manusmriti</b> gave the Chandalas the carrying away of corpses and the execution of condemned criminals.</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>The Brahmanical law books of this age, the Dharmashastras such as the <b>Manusmriti</b>, ranked people in four varnas fixed by birth: Brahmins, Kshatriyas, Vaishyas and Shudras, with groups such as the Chandalas placed outside them.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li></ul></section>
